2026-05-30 01:38:00.081  POST    node
  │  → body
  │  {
  │    "code": "BG",
  │    "name": "market_quarter",
  │    "label": "Bruges — Cloth Hall",
  │    "act": 2,
  │    "N": null,
  │    "S": null,
  │    "E": "LB",
  │    "W": null,
  │    "text": "The Cloth Hall of Bruges is the largest building in northern Europe that was not built for God or for war. It was built for wool. The belfry above it rings every hour to tell the weavers how much of their life has passed since dawn. The answer is always: more than they thought.\n\nBruges is where the Baltic trade meets the Atlantic trade. English wool comes in. Baltic amber and Riga fur go out. Venetian glass and Florentine banking instruments pass through in both directions. The money changers work in a street called the Beursplein — the word for stock exchange comes from a family name here, a family that hung a painted shield above their door and let merchants use their courtyard to make deals.\n\nThe Hanseatic kontor occupies a walled compound near the canal. They are guests in Bruges. They behave like landlords.",
