  │    "xpAward": 110,
  │    "vignetteText": "The road narrows at the mill bridge. Two men in the bishop's livery stand at the gate — not soldiers, functionaries, which is worse. Soldiers look at weapons. Functionaries look at faces. The pamphlet is inside your coat, folded twice, wrapped in oilcloth. It is the size of a prayer book. It is not a prayer book. They will recognize the difference if they look. You need them not to look.",
  │    "passText": "You hold their gaze and ask about the road ahead, whether the mud past the mill is as bad as everyone says, whether the bishop has men at the ford as well — all the while your coat does not move and your voice does not move and they decide you are boring and wave you through. The pamphlet crosses the checkpoint. The words in it will be read tonight in a back room by candlelight by people who have never heard the scripture in a language they understand.",
  │    "failText": "The functionary's eyes drop to your coat at the wrong moment. Not suspicion — reflex. But the outline is wrong. He says: open your coat, please. The please does not help. The pamphlet is taken. You are let go with a warning. The words do not cross today.",
